#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1059
msgid "Quick operational snapshot for the last 10 minutes. These counters are PHP-level event-log counters. If Apache/LiteSpeed .htaccess guard blocks a request before WordPress loads, the request is protected but cannot be counted here; use server access logs for those pre-PHP hits."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1060
msgid "Shows the latest internal health checks. These tests verify the homepage, the configured WooCommerce shop/category test path, protected filtered URLs, cookie behavior, XML-RPC blocking, and robots.txt availability."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1061
msgid "Recent allow/block/noindex/mode/test events. This section helps you confirm which URLs matched the policy, which action was taken, and whether traffic looks like normal filtering or automated crawling."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1062
msgid "Controls the main protection policy and the server-level guard. Start with Monitor Only to learn traffic patterns, use SEO Soft for indexing control, use Cookie mode for normal protection, and reserve Cookie + Referer, Strict, or Emergency for active floods."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1063
msgid "Defines how expensive a filtered archive URL is. More filter parameters, longer queries, and configured query keys increase the score. The score decides when cookies are required, when requests are blocked, and when emergency behavior should apply."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1064
msgid "Configures the lightweight first-party human cookie. Signed and rotating cookies make simple bot replay harder while keeping real visitors fast after their first valid page view. Very strict binding can affect visitors behind changing networks."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1065
msgid "Controls what the plugin records and how much personally identifiable data is stored. Hash or anonymize IPs for privacy, keep retention short, and enable sampling to avoid database pressure during large crawl floods."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1066
msgid "Automatically escalates protection when traffic volume crosses configured thresholds, then returns to the base recovery mode after the site is quiet. Tune thresholds carefully so seasonal traffic spikes do not trigger aggressive blocking."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1067
msgid "Optional PHP-level rate limiting using WordPress transients or object cache. It is useful as a safety net, but high-volume attacks are better handled with .htaccess, Nginx, Cloudflare, or other edge/server rules."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1068
msgid "Validates declared search bots before trusting their user-agent names. This protects against fake Googlebot/Bingbot strings while allowing verified crawlers to receive gentler handling where appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1069
msgid "Safety controls for rule deployment and cleanup. Health checks catch broken server rules after saving; rollback can restore the latest backup; uninstall cleanup controls whether generated files are removed when deleting the plugin."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1070
msgid "Copy-ready Apache/LiteSpeed, Nginx, and Cloudflare rules for environments where automatic file writing is not possible or where edge blocking is preferred. Apply only the rule set that matches your server/CDN."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1071
msgid "Lists backups created before the plugin changes managed files such as .htaccess, robots.txt, or blocked-light.html. Use these backups to recover quickly from a bad rule deployment."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1072
msgid "Manual maintenance tools for tests, rewriting/removing managed rules, restoring the latest backup, rotating cookie secrets, and exporting event logs. Use destructive actions carefully on production sites."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1073
msgid "Main behavior switch. Monitor Only is safest for observation; SEO Soft adds indexing controls; Cookie mode protects filtered URLs while allowing real visitors; Cookie + Referer is stricter; Strict/Emergency are high-impact blocking modes."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1074
msgid "The mode Auto Emergency returns to after the recovery period. Choose a stable everyday mode such as Monitor, SEO Soft, or Cookie; do not use Emergency as the recovery target."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1075
msgid "When enabled on Apache/LiteSpeed, the plugin writes rewrite rules that block expensive filtered URLs before WordPress/PHP loads. This is faster than PHP blocking but cannot honor WordPress role allowlists."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1076
msgid "Blocks xmlrpc.php at PHP and managed .htaccess levels. Enable this when XML-RPC is not needed for Jetpack, mobile publishing, or legacy integrations."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1077
msgid "HTTP response used for blocked requests. 403 clearly means forbidden; 404 hides the route from basic bots. Keep behavior consistent with your SEO and monitoring expectations."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1078
msgid "Regular expression for URL paths that should be protected, usually WooCommerce shop/category archives. Keep it narrow, for example product-category and shop paths, so normal pages are not affected."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1079
msgid "Regular expression for expensive query parameter names such as filter_*, query_type_*, price filters, ordering, stock status, and view changes. These keys identify layered-navigation crawl targets."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1080
msgid "Optional legacy cookie-name compatibility for PHP checks only. Generated server/CDN rules use the current FacetFence cookie names, and real validation still requires the signed HMAC cookie in PHP."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1081
msgid "IPs or CIDR/range patterns that bypass PHP-level checks. Use only for trusted admins, monitoring systems, or known internal networks. This does not bypass pre-PHP .htaccess blocking."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1082
msgid "Optional PHP-level bypass for trusted user agents. Keep this very narrow because user-agent strings are easy to spoof unless combined with verified-bot checks."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1083
msgid "Comma-separated WordPress role slugs allowed through PHP-level protection. This is useful for admins/shop managers, but server-level .htaccess rules run before WordPress knows the user role."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1084
msgid "Number of filter/query parameters allowed before the URL is considered too complex. Lower values are stricter; higher values allow deeper customer filtering but may increase crawl load."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1085
msgid "Maximum full query-string length before extra score is added. Very long query strings often indicate crawler-generated combinations or attack traffic."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1086
msgid "Score level where the plugin starts requiring a valid human cookie in cookie-based modes. Set below the block score so real users can be challenged before being blocked."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1087
msgid "Score level where the plugin blocks a request in blocking modes. Increase this if legitimate filter combinations are being blocked; decrease it during severe floods."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1088
msgid "Very high score threshold that marks a request as emergency-level complexity. Use this to catch extreme filter combinations even before volume-based thresholds trigger."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1089
msgid "In SEO Soft Mode, filter URLs up to this score can receive noindex/canonical handling while higher-score URLs can be redirected to a clean URL or blocked, depending on the selected action."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1090
msgid "Sets a lightweight first-party HttpOnly cookie for real visitors. Cookie-based protection depends on this unless another trusted flow sets a compatible cookie."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1091
msgid "Base name for the human cookie. Keep the default unless you have a naming conflict or need to coordinate with existing server/CDN rules."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1092
msgid "Cookie lifetime in seconds. Shorter TTLs reduce replay window; longer TTLs reduce repeat challenges for real shoppers."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1093
msgid "Adds HMAC signing, optional daily name rotation, and optional binding to User-Agent or IP prefix. Stronger binding improves abuse resistance but can affect visitors whose browser/network changes frequently."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1094
msgid "Enables recording of important plugin events such as blocked requests, allowed filtered requests, noindex decisions, mode changes, and self-tests."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1095
msgid "Where events are stored. Database is easiest to review in WordPress; NDJSON file is useful for server-side analysis; Both gives redundancy but writes more data."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1096
msgid "Controls IP privacy in logs. Hash only is privacy-friendly, anonymized keeps partial troubleshooting value, and full IP should be used only when you need exact forensic data."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1097
msgid "Number of days to keep logs before cleanup. Short retention reduces database size and privacy exposure; longer retention helps investigate recurring crawl patterns."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1098
msgid "Sampling rule for high traffic. After the configured events-per-minute threshold, the plugin records only one out of every N repetitive events while counters still drive protection decisions."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1099
msgid "Reduces repetitive per-request logging while Emergency mode is active. This protects the database and filesystem when the site is already under heavy crawl pressure."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1100
msgid "Turns on automatic escalation to Strict/Emergency based on request volume and IP diversity. Keep disabled if you prefer manual mode changes only."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1101
msgid "Rolling time window used to count filtered and blocked requests for Auto Emergency decisions. Shorter windows react faster; longer windows are smoother but slower."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1102
msgid "Number of matched requests within the window required to move into Strict mode. Set above normal peak traffic but below flood levels."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1103
msgid "Number of matched or blocked requests within the window required to move into Emergency mode. This should be higher than the Strict threshold."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1104
msgid "Minimum number of different IPs seen in the window before emergency escalation. Helps distinguish distributed crawl floods from one aggressive visitor or tool."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1105
msgid "Quiet period before Auto Emergency returns to the base recovery mode. Increase it if attacks come in waves; decrease it if you want faster recovery."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1106
msgid "Enables PHP-level rate limits. Useful for moderate abuse, but not a replacement for server/CDN protection during high-volume floods because WordPress must still load."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1107
msgid "Maximum matching requests from one IP within the rate-limit window before temporary blocking starts."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1108
msgid "Maximum matching requests from the same approximate network range within the window. Helps slow small bot clusters that rotate nearby IPs."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1109
msgid "Length of the rate-limit counting window in seconds. Shorter windows are more responsive; longer windows are more tolerant but less immediate."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1110
msgid "Temporary block duration after a rate limit is exceeded. Keep it long enough to cool down abuse but not so long that accidental blocks persist for hours."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1111
msgid "Select which search bot families to verify and whether failed verification should be blocked. Verification is important because bot user-agent names can be forged."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1112
msgid "How long successful bot verification is cached. Higher values reduce DNS/verification overhead; lower values refresh identity checks more often."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1113
msgid "Maximum complexity score allowed for verified bots before normal protection applies. Use this to keep legitimate crawlers from exploring extremely expensive filter combinations."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1114
msgid "Runs self-tests after writing managed files. If rollback is enabled and tests fail, the plugin restores the latest backup to avoid leaving broken blocking rules active."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1115
msgid "Clean WooCommerce shop or category path used for tests before filter parameters are added. It must return 200 so the plugin can distinguish real rule problems from missing pages."
msgstr ""

#: facetfence-product-filters.php:1116
msgid "Controls cleanup on plugin deletion. Enable when you want .htaccess, robots.txt, and blocked-light.html changes removed automatically during uninstall."
msgstr ""

